module

public import Mathlib
public import TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.AbelianVariety.Albanese

/-!
# Heights, rational points and obstructions, Part II: target signatures

The roadmap is README.md; this file is not the roadmap. It states headline theorems
against the pinned APIs. Uniform Manin–Mumford and Mordell–Lang for curves,
and the degree-free rational-point bound, use the native Albanese property and the group of points of a group object.
The genus is the dimension of the Albanese variety. Multiplication of points
in these signatures denotes the additive abelian-variety law. A translation
accounts for every degree-one divisor class. For the rational-point bound a
rational base point is chosen; a curve with no rational points has count zero.

The following headline theorems are not statable at the pins.
* The DGH level-case height inequality and its adelic comparison lack canonical
  heights for general polarized abelian schemes and Betti non-degeneracy.
* Family equidistribution lacks the invariant adelic measure and its Betti comparison.
* Uniform Bogomolov and the gap principle lack general abelian canonical heights,
  stable Faltings heights, polarization degrees and Ueno complements.
* Rémond's generalized Vojta and Mumford inequalities and his counting theorem
  lack general abelian canonical heights, cycle heights and difference-map fibres.
* The uniform count off the Ueno locus and uniform Mordell–Lang lack polarization
  degrees and the Ueno/coset interfaces on arbitrary abelian subvarieties.
* The Gao–Habegger and Xie–Yuan geometric Bogomolov theorems lack geometric trace,
  special subvarieties and canonical heights over polarized function fields.
* Yamaki's reduction lacks nonarchimedean abelian uniformization, the maximal
  nowhere-degenerate subvariety and the geometric trace quotient.
* Small-point bounds for potentially big families and the integral bound across
  characteristics lack adelic line bundles, potential bigness and restriction.
* Relative Bogomolov for the five curve-family maps lacks invariant adelic heights,
  the relative Jacobian maps and bigness over the field of constants.
* Yuan's uniform Bogomolov theorem over all global fields lacks twice-theta heights,
  stable Faltings heights and the isotrivial-pair interface on general Jacobians.
* Bielliptic uniform Bogomolov and unbounded torsion orders lack the
  diagonal-lift polarization and the elliptic quotient maps; bielliptic uniform
  Manin–Mumford is the genus-two case of the curve headline below.

Euclidean packing and the linear core of subvariety descent are supporting results
in README.md, rather than replacements for these headline statements.
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace TauCetiRoadmap.HeightsRationalPointsAndObstructionsPartII

universe u

open CategoryTheory MonoidalCategory AlgebraicGeometry TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry

open scoped CategoryTheory.MonObj

/-! ## Layer 3: Uniform Bogomolov and Manin–Mumford -/

/-- For each genus at least two, the number of torsion points on every translate
of an Abel–Jacobi curve over an algebraically closed characteristic-zero field
is bounded solely by the genus. The Albanese universal property identifies the
Jacobian and its Abel map. Kühne, Theorem 2. -/
theorem uniform_maninMumford_curves (g : ℕ) (hg : 2 ≤ g) :
    ∃ B : ℕ, 1 ≤ B ∧ ∀ (F : Type u) [Field F] [IsAlgClosed F] [CharZero F]
      (X : Over (Spec (.of F))) [Smooth X.hom] [IsProper X.hom]
      [GeometricallyIntegral X.hom] (_ : topologicalKrullDim X.left = 1)
      (x₀ : 𝟙_ (Over (Spec (.of F))) ⟶ X)
      (J : AbelianVariety F) (a : X ⟶ J.toOver),
      AbelianVariety.IsAlbanese x₀ a → J.dim = g →
      ∀ c : 𝟙_ (Over (Spec (.of F))) ⟶ J.toOver,
        {x : 𝟙_ (Over (Spec (.of F))) ⟶ X |
          IsOfFinOrder ((x ≫ a) * c)}.encard ≤ B := by
  sorry

/-! ## Layer 6: Uniform Mordell–Lang and rational points -/

/-- A translate of a genus-g Abel–Jacobi curve meets any subgroup of rank at
most ρ in at most B(g)^(1+ρ) points. Division-hull containment in a subgroup
generated by at most ρ points expresses the rank bound without finite generation
of the subgroup being counted. Kühne, Theorem 4. -/
theorem uniform_mordellLang_curves (g : ℕ) (hg : 2 ≤ g) :
    ∃ B : ℕ, 1 ≤ B ∧ ∀ (F : Type u) [Field F] [IsAlgClosed F] [CharZero F]
      (X : Over (Spec (.of F))) [Smooth X.hom] [IsProper X.hom]
      [GeometricallyIntegral X.hom] (_ : topologicalKrullDim X.left = 1)
      (x₀ : 𝟙_ (Over (Spec (.of F))) ⟶ X)
      (J : AbelianVariety F) (a : X ⟶ J.toOver),
      AbelianVariety.IsAlbanese x₀ a → J.dim = g →
      ∀ (c : 𝟙_ (Over (Spec (.of F))) ⟶ J.toOver) (ρ : ℕ)
        (s : Finset (𝟙_ (Over (Spec (.of F))) ⟶ J.toOver))
        (Γ : Subgroup (𝟙_ (Over (Spec (.of F))) ⟶ J.toOver)),
        s.card ≤ ρ →
        (∀ p ∈ Γ, ∃ n : ℕ, 0 < n ∧ p ^ n ∈ Subgroup.closure (s : Set _)) →
        {x : 𝟙_ (Over (Spec (.of F))) ⟶ X | (x ≫ a) * c ∈ Γ}.encard
          ≤ B ^ (1 + ρ) := by
  sorry

/-- For fixed genus g at least two, the number of rational points over every
number field is bounded by B(g)^(1+ρ) when the Jacobian point group has rank
at most ρ. A rational base point identifies the Albanese map; the empty
rational-point case has count zero. Kühne, Theorem 4 and the remark after it;
this implies Dimitrov–Gao–Habegger, Theorem 1.1. -/
theorem uniform_rationalPoints_curves (g : ℕ) (hg : 2 ≤ g) :
    ∃ B : ℕ, 1 ≤ B ∧ ∀ (F : Type u) [Field F] [NumberField F],
      ∀ (X : Over (Spec (.of F))) [Smooth X.hom] [IsProper X.hom]
        [GeometricallyIntegral X.hom] (_ : topologicalKrullDim X.left = 1)
        (x₀ : 𝟙_ (Over (Spec (.of F))) ⟶ X)
        (J : AbelianVariety F) (a : X ⟶ J.toOver),
        AbelianVariety.IsAlbanese x₀ a → J.dim = g →
        ∀ (ρ : ℕ) (s : Finset (𝟙_ (Over (Spec (.of F))) ⟶ J.toOver)),
          s.card ≤ ρ →
          (∀ p : 𝟙_ (Over (Spec (.of F))) ⟶ J.toOver,
            ∃ n : ℕ, 0 < n ∧ p ^ n ∈ Subgroup.closure (s : Set _)) →
          (Set.univ : Set (𝟙_ (Over (Spec (.of F))) ⟶ X)).encard
            ≤ B ^ (1 + ρ) := by
  sorry

end TauCetiRoadmap.HeightsRationalPointsAndObstructionsPartII
